---
import CmdbGraph from './CmdbGraph.astro';
import Terminal from './Terminal.astro';

// Copy is verbatim from the original. What changed is the composition: each study is
// now its own band with the evidence at large scale on an alternating side, rather than
// three identical prose blocks stacked in one column. The alternation is the point --
// three sections with the same silhouette read as a template no matter what they say.
const studies = [
  {
    id: 'sync',
    title: 'The sync job that took half a day',
    post: '/blog/the-sync-job-that-took-half-a-day',
    stack: ['Ruby on Rails', 'Sidekiq', 'PostgreSQL'],
    visual: 'runtime',
    body: [
      "AssetSonar's device sync was taking 12 to 15 hours to run. New hardware wouldn't show up for most of a working day, and customers noticed.",
      "The cause was the ordinary, expensive kind: N+1 queries and no batching, on a table that had been accumulating devices for years. I rebuilt the pipeline around batched writes and cut the redundant lookups.",
      "Under an hour now. The batching pattern stuck around a lot longer than the original fix did. Every MDM integration added since has been built on top of it.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'rmine',
    title: 'rmine',
    post: '/blog/designing-a-cli-for-agents',
    stack: ['Go', 'Claude Code SDK', 'Redmine API'],
    visual: 'terminal',
    proof: { label: 'github.com/mehboobali98/rmine', href: 'https://github.com/mehboobali98/rmine' },
    body: [
      "I wanted to know what five years of my own ticket history actually said. Nothing existing made that easy, so I wrote something: a Redmine CLI in Go, with a Claude Code skill built directly in, so an agent can query tickets and time logs on its own instead of me copy-pasting API responses into a prompt.",
      "Open source now. The rest of the team runs it day to day, and the numbers section above this one came straight out of it. Not a demo. Just what the tool actually found.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'cmdb',
    title: 'A CMDB that actually models relationships',
    stack: ['Ruby on Rails', 'PostgreSQL', 'Graph modeling'],
    visual: 'cmdb',
    proof: {
      label: 'Publicly documented by EZO',
      href: 'https://ezo.io/assetsonar/blog/import-cmdb-relationships-55b7baeda4d1/',
    },
    body: [
      "Most CMDBs are static. An asset points to a user, maybe a location, and that's about it. Anything past that takes a support ticket.",
      "We wanted something that could answer a real question: everything that touches this server, three hops out. I led the design and the build, a data model supporting n-level relationship traversal plus the ITSM-integrated UI on top of it. Seven months, architecture to production, leading a five-person team through it.",
      "Highest concentration of product-flagged Key Features of anything I shipped that year.",
    ],
  },
];

// Verbatim from `rmine --help` on the current build of the CLI.
const rmineHelp = [
  'A command-line client for Redmine',
  '',
  { text: 'Available Commands:', dim: true },
  '  issue       Work with Redmine issues',
  '  project     Browse Redmine projects',
  '  skill       Manage the rmine Claude Code skill',
  '  time        Log and manage time entries',
  "  whoami      Show the active profile's user",
  '',
  { text: 'Flags:', dim: true },
  '  -o, --output string    table|json',
  '      --profile string   profile to use',
];
---

<section id="work" class="bg-ink border-t border-line">
  <div class="band band--dense shell">
    <p class="marker mb-6">Selected work</p>
    <h2 class="t-display-2 text-paper max-w-[28ch]">
      Three things I'd actually want to talk about in an interview.
    </h2>
  </div>

  {studies.map((s, i) => (
    <div
      id={s.id}
      data-reveal
      class:list={['border-t border-line', i % 2 === 1 ? 'bg-surface' : 'bg-ink']}
    >
      <article class="band shell grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 lg:gap-20 items-center">
        <div class:list={['measure min-w-0', i % 2 === 1 && 'lg:order-2 lg:justify-self-end']}>
          <h3 class="t-display-3 text-paper">{s.title}</h3>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-4">
            {s.stack.map((t) => (
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] px-2.5 py-1 border border-line rounded-full text-quiet">{t}</span>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col gap-4">
            {s.body.map((p) => (
              <p class="text-paper/80 leading-relaxed">{p}</p>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col items-start gap-1">
            {s.post && (
              <a href={s.post} class="edge-link tap font-mono text-xs text-signal hover:underline">
                Read the long version &rarr;
              </a>
            )}
            {s.proof && (
              <a href={s.proof.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link tap">
                {s.proof.label} &rarr;
              </a>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class:list={['min-w-0', i % 2 === 1 && 'lg:order-1']}>
          {s.visual === 'runtime' && (
            <dl class="metric metric-pair">
              <div>
                <dt>Before</dt>
                <dd class="text-[2.5rem] lg:text-[3.25rem]">12&ndash;15 hours</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>After</dt>
                <dd class="text-[2.5rem] lg:text-[3.25rem]">Under 1 hour</dd>
              </div>
              <p class="metric-note w-full">
                Same job, same data volume. The batching pattern it produced is still what
                every MDM integration since has been built on.
              </p>
            </dl>
          )}
          {s.visual === 'terminal' && (
            <Terminal caption="rmine --help" command="rmine --help" output={rmineHelp} />
          )}
          {s.visual === 'cmdb' && <CmdbGraph />}
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  ))}
</section>
